AREAS=[
dict(slug="divorce",name="Divorce",img="signing.jpg",
card="Contested and uncontested divorce, property and debt division, alimony, and agreements that protect your financial future.",
tagline="Thoughtful guidance through the legal and financial decisions that come with ending a marriage.",
sections=[("Ending a marriage with clarity",[
"A divorce is two things at once: a legal case and one of the hardest personal transitions most people ever face. The legal side asks a district court to dissolve the marriage and decide how property, debts, support, and parenting will be handled. The personal side involves grief, uncertainty, and dozens of decisions that seem to arrive all at once. Good representation treats both seriously.",
"In Oklahoma, divorce is governed largely by Title 43 of the Oklahoma Statutes. Before filing, at least one spouse generally must have been a resident of the state for six months. Most divorces proceed on the no-fault ground of incompatibility, which means neither spouse has to prove wrongdoing to end the marriage."]),
("Property, debt, and support",[
"Oklahoma divides marital property under an equitable distribution approach. “Equitable” means fair under the circumstances, which is not always the same as an exact fifty-fifty split. Separating marital property from separate property (such as assets owned before the marriage or received by gift or inheritance) is often the most important financial work in a case, especially where accounts have been mixed over time.",
"Support alimony may be available where one spouse has a demonstrated need and the other has the ability to pay. Retirement accounts, business interests, and the family home all require careful valuation and, often, specialized court orders to divide correctly. A settlement that looks fair on paper can carry hidden tax or liquidity problems, so we review every proposal with long-term consequences in mind."]),
("Agreed or contested",[
"Many divorces settle. When spouses can agree, the case can move through a negotiated decree with far less cost and conflict. When there are minor children, Oklahoma generally applies a waiting period of ninety days after filing before a decree may be entered, and parents may be required to complete a parenting education program.",
"When agreement is not possible, a contested case involves temporary orders, financial disclosures, discovery, and possibly trial. Our approach is the same either way: understand the facts thoroughly, give you an honest assessment, and pursue the resolution that best protects your interests and your children."])],
pull="A fair settlement is not just about today's balance sheet. It is about whether you can rebuild comfortably in the years ahead.",
steps=[("Strategy session","We review your goals, finances, and any urgent safety or parenting concerns."),("Filing & temporary orders","We prepare the petition and request interim orders for support, custody, or the home if needed."),("Disclosure & negotiation","Both sides exchange financial information, and we negotiate or mediate toward agreement."),("Decree & follow-through","We finalize the decree and help with retirement division orders, title transfers, and next steps.")],
faqs=[("How long does a divorce take in Oklahoma?","It depends on whether the case is agreed or contested and whether there are minor children. Agreed cases without children can sometimes conclude quickly; cases with children are generally subject to a ninety-day waiting period, and contested cases can take many months."),
("Do I have to prove my spouse did something wrong?","Usually not. Most Oklahoma divorces use the no-fault ground of incompatibility. Conduct can still matter in some financial or parenting questions."),
("Will everything be split fifty-fifty?","Not necessarily. Oklahoma uses equitable distribution of marital property, which aims for fairness rather than a strict equal split. Separate property is generally treated differently."),
("Can we use one lawyer for both of us?","A lawyer can represent only one spouse. The other spouse may choose to represent themselves or hire separate counsel, and mediation is another way to reach agreement.")]),
dict(slug="child-custody",name="Child Custody",img="family-beach.jpg",
card="Parenting plans, custody and visitation, relocation, and modifications centered on your child's stability and well-being.",
tagline="Parenting plans designed around your child's real life: school, activities, relationships, and stability.",
sections=[("The child's best interests come first",[
"Every Oklahoma custody decision rests on one central question: what arrangement serves the best interests of the child? Courts consider a range of factors, including each parent's relationship with the child, the ability to provide a stable home, the child's adjustment to school and community, and each parent's willingness to support the child's relationship with the other parent.",
"Oklahoma law expresses a policy of assuring that children have frequent and continuing contact with both parents when that is in the child's best interests. That policy does not mean every family ends up with an identical schedule. It means the court starts from the child's needs and builds outward."]),
("Legal custody, physical custody, and parenting time",[
"Custody has two parts. Legal custody concerns decision-making on major issues such as education, health care, and religious upbringing. Physical custody and parenting time concern where the child lives and how time is shared. Joint custody arrangements usually require a detailed joint custody plan that spells out how parents will communicate and resolve disagreements.",
"A strong parenting plan anticipates the ordinary friction points: holidays, summer breaks, transportation, extracurricular costs, communication with the child when they are with the other parent, and what happens when schedules change as children grow."]),
("When circumstances change",[
"Custody orders can be modified when there has been a material change in circumstances and a change would serve the child's best interests. Common reasons include a parent's proposed relocation, a change in the child's needs, or concerns about safety. Oklahoma has specific notice requirements for relocating a child's principal residence, and missing them can have real consequences.",
"Where there is a history of domestic abuse, the analysis changes significantly, and safety planning becomes part of the legal strategy from the first meeting."])],
pull="The best parenting plans are written for the child you actually have, not an idealized family on a calendar template.",
steps=[("Listen & assess","We learn about your child, your schedule, and any concerns about safety or stability."),("Build the plan","We draft a detailed parenting plan proposal grounded in the best-interest factors."),("Mediate or litigate","We pursue agreement through mediation where appropriate and present a clear case in court where needed."),("Adjust over time","We help with enforcement and modifications as your child grows and circumstances change.")],
faqs=[("Does Oklahoma favor mothers in custody cases?","Oklahoma law directs courts to decide custody based on the child's best interests and does not prefer a parent because of gender."),
("At what age can a child choose where to live?","A child does not simply choose. Oklahoma law allows the court to consider the preference of a child of sufficient age to form an intelligent preference, and there is a presumption that a child twelve or older is of sufficient age, but the decision remains the court's."),
("Can I move out of state with my child?","Relocation is governed by specific notice and objection procedures. Moving without following them can affect custody. Talk with an attorney before making plans."),
("What if the other parent ignores the schedule?","Court-ordered parenting time can be enforced. Keeping a calm, factual record of missed exchanges is often the first step.")]),
dict(slug="child-support",name="Child Support",img="law-books.jpg",
card="Establishing, calculating, modifying, and enforcing child support under Oklahoma's guidelines, with clear numbers and no surprises.",
tagline="Accurate calculations, fair outcomes, and practical help when support needs to change or be enforced.",
sections=[("How support is calculated",[
"Oklahoma uses statewide child support guidelines based on an income-shares model. The idea is straightforward: estimate what the parents together would have spent on the child if the household had stayed intact, then divide that obligation between the parents in proportion to their incomes.",
"The details are where cases are won or lost. Determining each parent's gross income can be complicated when someone is self-employed, earns commissions, or is underemployed. Adjustments may apply for the number of overnights each parent has, health insurance premiums, child care costs, and other children a parent supports."]),
("Beyond the base amount",[
"Support orders typically address more than a monthly payment. Medical support, the division of uninsured medical expenses, child care, and sometimes extracurricular costs are part of a complete order. Leaving these questions vague invites conflict later.",
"Oklahoma Human Services runs the state's child support services program, which can help establish paternity, set orders, and collect payments. Private counsel can work alongside or independently of that program depending on the family's needs."]),
("Modification and enforcement",[
"Support can be modified when there is a material change in circumstances, such as a significant change in income or in the child's needs. Modifications generally apply going forward, so acting promptly matters.",
"Unpaid support is a national problem. U.S. Census Bureau data show custodial parents received $19.2 billion of the $29.9 billion due in 2022. Enforcement tools can include income withholding and other remedies available through the courts and the state program."])],
pull="Child support is not a reward or a penalty. It is a calculation, and calculations should be done carefully.",
steps=[("Gather income records","We collect pay stubs, tax returns, and expense documentation for both households."),("Run the guidelines","We prepare a guideline calculation and identify any adjustments or disputes."),("Agree or present","We negotiate an agreed order or present the numbers clearly to the court."),("Monitor & modify","We help enforce the order or seek modification when circumstances materially change.")],
faqs=[("Can parents agree to no child support?","Courts generally review support agreements against the guidelines to protect the child. A deviation typically requires justification the court accepts."),
("Does shared parenting time reduce support?","Oklahoma's guidelines include a shared parenting adjustment when a parent has a substantial number of overnights, which can affect the amount."),
("What if I lose my job?","Contact an attorney or the state program promptly. A modification generally does not reach back before the request was filed."),
("How long does child support last?","Generally until the child turns eighteen, or longer in some circumstances such as a child still regularly attending high school, subject to statutory limits.")]),
dict(slug="mediation",name="Mediation",img="bg/twilight.jpg",
card="Structured, confidential negotiation that helps families resolve parenting and property issues without a courtroom battle.",
tagline="A calmer, more private way to reach agreements your family can live with.",
sections=[("What mediation is, and is not",[
"Mediation is a confidential process in which a neutral mediator helps the parties negotiate their own agreement. The mediator does not decide the case or give either side legal advice. Instead, they structure the conversation, help each side understand the other's priorities, and test proposals for workability.",
"For many families, mediation produces better outcomes than litigation: lower cost, faster resolution, more privacy, and agreements tailored to the family rather than imposed by a court calendar. Parents who build a plan together are often better positioned to co-parent afterward."]),
("Our role in mediation",[
"Sampson Family Law (in this fictional scenario) can serve in two roles. As your advocate, we prepare you for mediation, attend sessions with you or consult between them, and review any proposed agreement before you sign. As a neutral mediator, we would help both parties reach agreement and would not represent either one.",
"Preparation is what makes mediation work. That means organized financial information, a clear sense of priorities, and realistic knowledge of what a court would likely do if the case did not settle."]),
("When mediation may not be appropriate",[
"Mediation depends on both people being able to negotiate safely and freely. Where there is a history of domestic violence, coercive control, or a serious power imbalance, mediation may be inappropriate or may require special safeguards such as separate rooms. Oklahoma courts recognize these concerns, and they should be raised early.",
"Mediation also works best when both sides are willing to disclose financial information honestly. Where that is not happening, formal discovery through the court may need to come first."])],
pull="In mediation, the family keeps the pen. In court, a stranger writes the ending.",
steps=[("Intake & screening","We confirm mediation is safe and appropriate for your situation."),("Preparation","We organize finances, priorities, and realistic outcomes before the first session."),("Sessions","The mediator guides structured negotiation, together or in separate rooms."),("Agreement & filing","The agreement is reviewed, finalized, and submitted to the court for approval.")],
faqs=[("Is mediation required in Oklahoma?","Courts can order mediation in many family cases, particularly custody disputes, subject to exceptions such as domestic violence concerns."),
("Is what I say in mediation confidential?","Mediation communications are generally confidential under Oklahoma law, with limited exceptions. Ask about the specific rules before your session."),
("Do I still need a lawyer if we mediate?","A mediator cannot advise either party. Many people benefit from independent legal advice before signing an agreement."),
("What happens if mediation fails?","The case continues through the court process. Partial agreements can still narrow the issues for trial.")]),
dict(slug="family-law",name="Family Law",img="hero-tulsa-skyline.jpg",
card="Paternity, adoption, guardianship, prenuptial agreements, and protective orders, with steady counsel for every stage of family life.",
tagline="Comprehensive counsel for the legal moments that shape a family, from new beginnings to difficult transitions.",
sections=[("More than divorce",[
"Family law covers the legal relationships that define a household: marriage and its dissolution, parentage, custody, support, adoption, guardianship, and protection from abuse. In Oklahoma, most of these matters are governed by Title 43 and related statutes and are heard in district court.",
"Many families need help with something other than divorce. An unmarried father may need to establish paternity to secure parenting time. A grandparent may need guardianship to enroll a grandchild in school. A couple may want a prenuptial agreement before combining finances."]),
("Paternity, adoption, and guardianship",[
"Establishing paternity creates legal rights and responsibilities for both the parent and the child, including support, inheritance, and parenting time. It can be done by acknowledgment or through a court action, sometimes with genetic testing.",
"Adoption creates a permanent legal parent-child relationship, whether through a stepparent, relative, or agency placement. Guardianship gives a caregiver legal authority over a minor without terminating the parents' rights. Each process has its own requirements, home studies, and notice rules."]),
("Agreements and protection",[
"Prenuptial and postnuptial agreements let couples decide in advance how property and support will be handled. They are most durable when both parties make full financial disclosure and have time to consider the terms.",
"When safety is at risk, Oklahoma's Protection from Domestic Abuse Act provides a path to protective orders. Emergency orders may be available quickly, followed by a full hearing. Safety comes first, and advocates and hotlines are available around the clock."])],
pull="Families change shape. The law should help them do it with dignity and security.",
steps=[("Identify the issue","We clarify exactly what legal relationship or protection you need."),("Plan the route","We explain the process, documents, and timeline for your matter."),("File & advocate","We prepare filings, coordinate with agencies, and represent you at hearings."),("Secure the result","We confirm final orders are entered correctly and explain what they mean.")],
faqs=[("Do unmarried fathers have custody rights?","Parentage generally must be legally established before a father can seek court-ordered custody or parenting time."),
("Are prenuptial agreements enforceable in Oklahoma?","They can be, especially when entered voluntarily with fair disclosure. Courts look carefully at how the agreement was made."),
("What is the difference between guardianship and adoption?","Guardianship grants a caregiver legal authority without ending the parents' rights; adoption permanently creates a new legal parent-child relationship."),
("How quickly can I get a protective order?","Emergency ex parte orders may be issued quickly, with a full hearing scheduled later. If you are in danger, call 911.")]),
dict(slug="criminal-law",name="Criminal Defense & DUI",img="gavel.jpg",
card="DUI and criminal defense, including where criminal and family matters intersect: protective orders, domestic charges, and custody.",
tagline="Coordinated defense when a criminal matter overlaps with divorce, custody, or a protective order.",
sections=[("Where criminal and family law meet",[
"Family disputes and criminal charges often collide. An arrest during a domestic argument, a protective order filed alongside a divorce, or allegations raised in a custody dispute can create parallel cases in different courts with different rules, deadlines, and standards of proof.",
"What you say in one case can affect the other. A statement made in a family court hearing may be used in a criminal prosecution, and a plea in criminal court can shape custody decisions. Coordinating strategy across both matters is essential."]),
("Common overlapping matters",[
"The matters that most often overlap with family cases include domestic assault and battery charges, violations of protective orders, and child endangerment allegations. Each carries potential consequences beyond the criminal sentence, including effects on firearm rights, employment, and parenting time.",
"Protective orders under Oklahoma's Protection from Domestic Abuse Act are civil, but violating one can be a crime. Anyone served with an order should read it carefully and follow it exactly while the case proceeds."]),
("Our focus",[
"In this fictional practice, criminal work covers DUI defense and matters connected to family cases. A first DUI arrest in Oklahoma can involve two tracks at once: the criminal case and a separate driver's license matter with its own short deadlines. Anyone facing charges should speak with a licensed criminal defense attorney promptly; public defenders are available for those who qualify."])],
pull="When two courts are looking at the same family, one coordinated strategy matters more than two separate ones.",
steps=[("Immediate review","We review charges, orders, and upcoming dates in every related case."),("Coordinate counsel","We align strategy with criminal defense counsel where appropriate."),("Protect your rights","We prepare for hearings with attention to statements and evidence across cases."),("Plan ahead","We address how outcomes may affect custody, support, and future orders.")],
faqs=[("Should I talk to the police about a family dispute?","You have the right to remain silent and to consult a lawyer. Speak with an attorney before giving statements about pending matters."),
("Does a protective order affect custody?","It can. Protective orders may include temporary custody and contact provisions and can be considered in later custody decisions."),
("Can a criminal charge be used against me in divorce?","Conduct and charges can be relevant, especially to custody. Coordinated advice helps avoid unintended consequences."),
("Do you handle DUI cases?","Yes, in this demonstration. Deadlines on the license side can be short, so it is worth talking to counsel quickly.")]),
]

POSTS=[
dict(slug="preparing-for-a-divorce-consultation",title="Preparing for Your First Divorce Consultation",cat="Divorce",read=5,img="signing.jpg",
dek="What to bring, what to ask, and how to make the most of your first meeting with a family law attorney.",
body='''<p>The first meeting with a family law attorney can feel overwhelming. A little preparation turns it from an anxious conversation into a productive planning session.</p>
<h2>Bring the paperwork you have</h2><p>You do not need a perfect file. Recent pay stubs, the last two years of tax returns, statements for bank and retirement accounts, mortgage and loan statements, and any court papers you have received are a strong start. If you have children, a rough sketch of the current weekly schedule helps too.</p>
<h2>Write down your goals and worries</h2><p>Before the meeting, list what matters most to you: staying in the house, a particular parenting schedule, protecting a business, or simply ending things quickly. Then list your biggest fears. A good attorney will address both.</p>
<h2>Questions worth asking</h2><ul><li>What are the likely paths for a case like mine, and how long does each take?</li><li>What decisions do I need to make soon, and which can wait?</li><li>How are fees structured, and what would a realistic budget look like?</li><li>What should I avoid doing while the case is pending?</li></ul>
<h2>Think about safety and privacy</h2><p>If you share devices or accounts with your spouse, consider how you will communicate privately with your attorney. If you have any safety concerns, raise them at the very beginning of the meeting.</p>
<blockquote>Preparation does not mean having every answer. It means arriving ready to ask the right questions.</blockquote>'''),
dict(slug="understanding-oklahoma-child-support",title="Understanding Child Support in Oklahoma",cat="Child Support",read=6,img="law-books.jpg",
dek="A plain-English look at how Oklahoma's income-shares guidelines work and why the details matter.",
body='''<p>Child support often feels like a mysterious number produced by a computer. In reality, Oklahoma's guidelines follow a logical framework, and understanding it helps parents spot errors and plan ahead.</p>
<h2>The income-shares idea</h2><p>Oklahoma's guidelines start by combining both parents' incomes and estimating what an intact household at that income level would typically spend on a child. That total obligation is then divided between the parents in proportion to their incomes.</p>
<h2>What gets added or adjusted</h2><p>Health insurance premiums for the child, work-related child care, and the number of overnights each parent has can all change the result. Income disputes, particularly for self-employed parents, are often the biggest source of disagreement.</p>
<h2>The national picture</h2><p>According to the U.S. Census Bureau, custodial parents were due $29.9 billion in child support in 2022 and received $19.2 billion. That gap is one reason enforcement tools exist. See the <a href="../evidence.html">evidence page</a> for sources.</p>
<h2>When things change</h2><p>A significant change in income or in a child's needs may justify a modification. Because changes generally apply only from the date a request is filed, waiting can be costly.</p>
<p>Oklahoma Human Services offers an <a href="https://oklahoma.gov/okdhs/services/child-support-services.html">official child support services program</a> that can help establish, modify, and enforce orders.</p>'''),
dict(slug="co-parenting-through-the-holidays",title="Co-Parenting Through the Holidays",cat="Child Custody",read=4,img="gathering-place.jpg",
dek="Practical ways separated parents can reduce conflict and protect children's joy during the busiest season of the year.",
body='''<p>Holidays magnify everything about co-parenting: the logistics, the emotions, and the stakes for children who want to enjoy the season without feeling caught in the middle.</p>
<h2>Read your parenting plan early</h2><p>Many disputes start because someone assumed the schedule rather than checking it. Review holiday provisions weeks in advance, confirm exchange times in writing, and clarify any ambiguity calmly before plans are made.</p>
<h2>Put the child's experience first</h2><p>Children remember how the holidays felt more than exactly where they spent each day. Avoid scheduling that turns the season into a series of rushed handoffs, and resist the urge to compete with gifts.</p>
<h2>Communicate like colleagues</h2><p>Keep messages brief, factual, and polite. Co-parenting apps can help by keeping a clear record and reducing the temptation to relitigate old arguments.</p>
<h2>Know when to get help</h2><p>If the other parent repeatedly ignores the schedule, or if there are safety concerns, document what happens and talk to an attorney about enforcement or modification options.</p>
<blockquote>The best holiday gift for a child of separated parents is permission to enjoy both homes.</blockquote>'''),
]

